hello let's talk religion before we  start any kind of discussion on this  channel we should probably get rid of  the elephant in the room I mean if we're  going to talk about religion that we  should probably all agree on how to  define the word itself right well turns  out that's a very difficult thing to do  when by the end of this video you might  just be more confused and you were going  in which I guess in a way defeats the  purpose really but anyway I should point  out that there's a great video on this  very subject over on the religion for  breakfast channel and it's actually  really good so after you watch this one  you should go and give him some love as  well I'll leave a link in the  description okay so in the academic  study of religions we paradoxically used  the word religion with great caution one  of the first thing we learn when we  study religion is that there is no  official definition for the word and you  can obnoxiously type in some definition  that you've heard or say that our  religion is opium for the masses but the  fact remains that there is no official  definition for the word that doesn't  mean the people haven't tried however  for example Edward B Taylor who is  considered one of the founders of  anthropology the defined religion as  belief in supernatural beings and this  is of course a rather narrow and  specific definition for the word that  also leaves out a lot of what we would  call religion today  another example is by Clifford Geertz  who has a much more broad definition for  the word he writes that religion is a  system of symbols which acts to  establish powerful pervasive and  long-lasting moods and motivations in  men by formulating conceptions of a  general order of existence and clothing  these conceptions with such an aura of  factuality that the moods and motivation  seems uniquely realistic now these  obviously are two very different ideas  about what religion is and who's to say  that one is more right or wrong than the  other  even if these two individuals are  greatly admired and followed by modern  anthropologists everyone still creates  their own image and idea of what  religion is in other ways we create  our own field so to say consider this  painting for example is this religion or  is it arts if your answer is that it's  both then which part of it is religion  and which part of it is arts can you  divide it up into these two categories  it kind of blends together doesn't it I  think we would all agree that  Christianity is a religion right but is  Scientology religion is Jediism a  religion is football a religion even it  all depends on how you define the word  religion itself many scholars have tried  to get to the bottom of the question of  religion especially in the last century  or so and there are a lot of conflicting  opinions about what sort of the  underlying causes of it are for example  Emile Durkheim is one of the champions  of a sociological explanation for  religion and he argues that the idea of  society is the soul of religion to him  religion is a set of practices and  symbols that work mainly towards social  cohesion much like a nation's flag  religion becomes a symbol for a certain  group of people and a kind of uniting  principle he studied Australian tribes  and so-called simple peoples basically  people that live like we would imagine  people lived thousands of years ago and  he studied totem culture and told him  religion in these tribal cultures in  these studies he shows that different  totems represent different tribes or  different groups and is also worshipped  by those same groups by extension they  are worshipping themselves or more  specifically the tribe to which they  belong and Durkheim extends this idea to  other religions and behavior outside  religion as well so for example if you  have a nation like the u.s. there are  certain symbols that come to mind like  the flag with with the stars a hamburger  maybe or a bald eagle and now the bald  eagle for instance becomes a symbol for  the US and for American patriotism  religions to Durkheim works the same way  except it is the god or the deity or  whatever it is that becomes that uniting  and symbolic principle that that the  group can sort of unite under another  well-known theory is a more  psychological perspective  by people like Sigmund Freud who argued  that religion is a faulty sense of  security for people much like a teddy  bear for a child and that we should get  rid of that teddy bear even compares  religion to certain mental illnesses and  argues that quote religion is comparable  to childhood neuroses he didn't like  religion very much these are just a few  examples of the ideas and theories on  the origin and explanation for religion  but there's a problem all of these  individual scholars academics have one  thing in common and that is that they  are all Western scholars working  primarily from a European or American  framework about things like what even  religion means and this is very  problematic in fact a very term religion  as we know it was probably conceived of  in this particular region in Europe in  America and only in the last few  centuries or so let me explain if I were  to ask you what the most central aspect  of religion is the core principle  throughout religion chances are you  would answer that it deals with faith or  some kind of belief or belief system and  if this is your answer then you're  probably from a Western Protestant  Christian culture in fact the idea of  religion is primarily faith is a very  Protestant idea and if you were to ask a  Hindu or a Muslim from India the same  question chances are they would give you  a lot different answers perhaps they say  religion is about a practice or certain  behavior or family even is a very good  example for this phenomenon and one that  my old teacher used to bring up all the  time so I'm from Sweden right and we  like to consider ourself sort of  secularized or not very religious in his  example he always brings up that in a  lot of cases when people come from other  parts of the world to Sweden they are  amazed to find out how religious we are  and now you may be confused and ask well  how are we religious well according to  them we take care of the elderly we  treat each other well we have a good  welfare system these kinds of things  which shows that to them religion is  completely different  their definition then what some of ours  maybe we think about belief in a God we  think about going to church they think  about welfare and taking care of the  elderly so completely different ideas  about what being religious means in fact  many argue that religion as we  understand it in the West is very much  based on Christianity and the templates  that Christianity contains because when  the Christian empires conquer the world  they often took very complex local  systems and traditions and placed the  label of religion on them you know that  thing that Christianity is only not as  good as an example of this you can just  look at the education about religion in  Europe and the textbooks that are given  to the students in them all religions  are often divided into three categories  according to a scholar who uses these a  lot of the time and these three  categories are the man the text and the  faith sound familiar  well this is because this is a Christian  template there is Jesus the man the  Bible the text and the Christian faith  the Christian theology that that the  core of Christianity the problem arrives  when I try to forcibly apply these  categories to other religions as if they  were the same when they are clearly not  the same even a closely related religion  like Islam when you put these categories  it is the man which becomes Mohammed the  text the Quran and the Muslim faith the  Muslim theology but the Quran is not  just the Muslim Bible the Quran has a  much different role and position within  Islam than the Bible has in Christianity  and Muhammad has a much different role  in Islam than Jesus has in Christianity  so to say that things like what the  Quran equals the Muslim Bible is very  problematic and that undermines the  complexity and an difference of of all  these traditions that were trying to  educate and study to further destroy an  idea of what religion is is to talk  about the apparent difference between  religion and the rest of society so let  me explain in the West today we have  certain category certain pockets of our  lives so there's politics there  sports there is culture and there's  religion and all these occupy their own  parts of our lives even if they blend  together sometimes but again this is a  very modern and Western perspective on  it this has not always been the case and  in a lot of parts of the world it still  isn't the case previously in history and  indeed in parts of the world still today  all these categories tended to blend  together it was all just part of life  religion didn't have its own place that  you could choose to approach or ignore  it was just there infinitely fused into  all aspects of life and love society but  this also means that our societies are a  lot more religious or affected by  religion than we tend to believe again  in Sweden we consider ourselves to be  very secular we have separated church  and state for example and and so on but  we don't realize how Christian we really  are everything about our culture just  fumes with Christianity our flag has a  cross on it we have our weekends on  Sundays and Saturdays we tend to believe  that religion is primarily about faith  or belief and our entire moral compass  is based on Christianity every part of  our society is in one way or another at  least partly influenced by the Christian  tradition to elaborate one of the most  famous scholars of religion Talal Asad  argues that there can be no distinction  between culture and religion in a famous  essay called the construction of  religion as an anthropological category  he argues that religion as a phenomenon  separated from politics law and science  have emerged from a unique Western  post-reformation history in other words  religion cannot be separated from  culture or any of the other categories  mentioned above as this is a construct  by the West and made to benefit the  Western or Christian worldview well not  everyone agrees with Assad and certainly  I think there is some value in using the  word religion his idea certainly point  to the fact that these lines between a  lot of these subjects are often very  fuzzy and  and really undefined of course our  discipline is called comparative  religion after all so the word obviously  means something you can even argue that  all religions around the world have  something in common that makes them  religions but this is also a very  dangerous path filled with snakes and  sharks and killer whales that will kill  you and play with your dead body it is  after all a word that exists for a  reason and it kind of means something I  mean if I say the word religion most of  you know at least somewhat what I'm  talking about and to sit here and try to  define words until they no longer mean  anything is to me just counterproductive  and I argue that we should use this word  both in conversation and as an academic  concept we should do so with great  caution and have all these things that  I've talked about in mind it is useful  as an analytical tool but it's also very  dangerous term and it tries to encompass  a whole lot I would argue that this is  religion versus religion this is  religion this is religion as well but  also this this and this religion says we  understand them exists all around the  world and in countless different forms  they can serve as justification for  terrible things but also of wonderful  and beautiful things if we accept a  broad definition of religion it  encompasses an almost endless number of  things but that's also what makes it so  interesting I'll see you next time  [Music]
 

